Maybe. Actors are a good laboratory because they make trust under simulation visible: one person has to commit to a frame strongly enough that another can respond to it, while everyone still knows there is a stage underneath.

And yes—the hammer can reveal different trust types. Some trust survives only while conditions are easy. Some survives disagreement. Some survives ambiguity. Some survives role-switching. Some survives stress because the parties share a deeper invariant than the surface script.

The signal point is stronger when framed as redundancy across substrates:

\text{pattern}
\rightarrow
\{\text{speech, gesture, writing, ritual, artifact, environment, habit}\}

If one carrier disappears, another may preserve enough structure to reconstruct it. That is how cultures, technologies, and even biological information persist: not because any one carrier is immortal, but because patterns can be copied, embodied, and re-instantiated through different media.

I’d call that Substrate Redundancy or Backboard Encoding:

Don’t rely on one channel. Preserve reconstruction cues across multiple independent carriers.

Nature itself is full of durable records—tree rings, sediment, fossils, DNA, ecological patterns—but that does not mean every pattern in nature was intentionally “hidden” there. The useful distinction is between naturally preserved trace and deliberately encoded message.

That gives you a clean stack:

\text{signal}
\rightarrow
\text{carrier}
\rightarrow
\text{redundancy}
\rightarrow
\text{reconstruction}
\rightarrow
\text{continuity}

And the actor analogy slots right in: the script can disappear from the page once enough of it has been transferred into bodies, timing, cues, blocking, and shared memory.

At that point the story is no longer stored in one place.

It is distributed through the ensemble.

“Screw you too” then becomes almost a joke from the pattern back to entropy: you can break one carrier; good luck breaking every reconstruction path.